ArduinoISP -> ATtiny85 (simulated ISP programming)
==================================================

An Arduino Uno runs ArduinoISP and programs a blank ATtiny85 through its
ISP pins:  D10 -> RESET (PB5),  D11 -> MOSI (PB0),  D12 <- MISO (PB1),
D13 -> SCK (PB2). A SerialPort component in TCP_Server mode links the
Arduino UART to 127.0.0.1:5333 so a real avrdude can talk to it:

  avrdude -c stk500v1 -P net:127.0.0.1:5333 -b 19200 -p t85 -U flash:w:blink_t85.hex:i

Or right click the Arduino -> "Program target via ISP (STK500v1)...".
After programming the ATtiny85 runs blink_t85.hex (PB3/PB4 toggle every 10 ms).
See docs/ISP.md (Spanish) for the full guide.

Files
-----
ArduinoISP.ino       ArduinoISP sketch, unmodified, from
                     https://github.com/arduino/arduino-examples
                     (examples/11.ArduinoISP/ArduinoISP/ArduinoISP.ino, main
                     branch fetched 2026-10-27, sha256 9d6318e03dce7c12825fed3abe82a6c8a6d16842d2b38d64b278c37844d011e2)
ArduinoISP.hex       That sketch built for Arduino Uno (ATmega328P, 16 MHz) with
                     build_arduinoisp.sh, avr-gcc 7.3 and the Arduino AVR core
                     1.8.6 (Debian package arduino-core-avr).
build_arduinoisp.sh  Rebuilds ArduinoISP.hex:  sh build_arduinoisp.sh ArduinoISP.ino ArduinoISP.hex
blink_t85.c/.hex     Test firmware for the ATtiny85 (avr-gcc -mmcu=attiny85 -Os).

